# Setup incomplete: staged setup tasks

This file was written by the setup wizard on {{DATE}}. It is the to-do list
for the AI assistant's setup sessions in this project. Setup is staged over
more than one session, with asynchronous work between sessions, so no single
sitting drags. Work the tasks in order, mark each one done here as it
completes, and delete this file only when every task is done.

Tasks marked (with user) need {{USER_NAME}} present. Tasks marked (async) do
not: run them between sessions, in parallel subagents where that helps, and
mark their outputs "draft, awaiting review" until {{USER_NAME}} has confirmed
them in the next session.

## Tasks

### Session A: intake (with user)

1. Before the first question, set expectations as "Before the first question"
   in `core/intake.md` describes: Jawbs is only as good as what
   {{USER_NAME}} shares about their career, their history and what they want
   next. Then run the intake interview in `core/intake.md` and record the
   answers there.
   Before the session ends, mine the conversation itself for `core/voice.md`
   and for the leads that seed `core/stories.md`: the intake is the richest
   sample of {{USER_NAME}} the project will get, and it is worth more than a
   writing sample that may never arrive. Both files explain how.
2. Build `core/profile.md`, including a fit bar appropriate to someone who is
   {{EMPLOYMENT_STATUS}}: what a new role must offer before it is worth moving for.
3. Ask {{USER_NAME}} to drop their existing CV (any format) into
   `core/source/`, and confirm the target directions the field research
   should cover (intake question 2).

### Between sessions (async)

4. Synthesise a draft `core/master-cv.md` from the source CV. Always produce
   two outputs: the best draft the material supports, and a gap list of what
   the source could not tell you (roles without outcomes, missing dates, thin
   evidence, unexplained periods). Never pad gaps with invention.
5. Research {{USER_NAME}}'s target field and draft `core/industry-brief.md`:
   how hiring works there, what recruiters prioritise, CV and letter
   conventions, market state. Follow the guidance at the top of that file.
6. Confirm the tracker works: read and write `tracker/applications.csv` as
   described in `tracker/tracker.md`.

   Tasks 4 and 5 are independent, so they suit parallel subagents; review
   any subagent output for accuracy before it reaches {{USER_NAME}}.

### Session B: review (with user)

7. Walk {{USER_NAME}} through the master CV draft, then offer a career-step
   interview for each role in it, most recent first, as "Career-step
   interviews" in `core/intake.md` describes. Use the gap list to steer the
   questions for each role. {{USER_NAME}} can take each interview, skip a
   role, or stop and carry on another day; this task is done when every role
   in the master CV is marked `Interviewed` or `Skipped by choice`.
8. Present the industry brief for review; it steers nothing until
   {{USER_NAME}} corrects and confirms it.
9. Extend `core/stories.md` and `core/voice.md` with what the career-step
   interviews add, and check both with {{USER_NAME}}. They were seeded from the intake
   in session A, so the voice notes describe how {{USER_NAME}} speaks: read
   the traits back and ask what is wrong. If a sample of their own writing
   exists, revise against it now.

### Session C: finishing (with user)

10. Choose the design and render a test CV and letter. The project ships with a default
    CV and cover letter design in `render/templates/`, and any template in
    Typst Universe's CV category can replace it:
    https://typst.app/universe/search/?kind=templates&category=cv
    - Before rendering, share that link and ask {{USER_NAME}} whether they
      would like to pick a design now or see the default first.
    - Render a test CV from `core/master-cv.md`: copy the skeleton
      `render/templates/configuration.yaml` to `applications/test-render/cv.yaml`,
      fill it from the master CV. Also copy `render/templates/cover-letter.yaml`
      to `applications/test-render/cover-letter.yaml` and fill it with a short
      sample letter from the master CV (long enough to fill about two thirds of
      the page, as `render/README.md` explains). Run
      `render/render.sh test-render`, check the CV fits one page, and show both
      PDFs to {{USER_NAME}}.
    - Then ask whether they are happy with the look or would like to pick
      another from the link.
    A pick at either point follows "Adapting a template" in `render/README.md`,
    then renders the test CV and letter again in the new design.
11. Delete this file and tell {{USER_NAME}} the project is ready to judge
    fits and draft applications.

